**Some relevant follow-up info and measurements:**

I've implemented a webGL2 version of the code and uploaded to an MIT-licensed repo for inspection https://github.com/0scarB/balls-collision-demo/blob/1a3ebe13fd2c8617eeac496f481be802977febf2/index.js#L32-L152.

You can view and profile the demo running on Github Pages https://0scarb.github.io/balls-collision-demo.

To compare performance of the canvas 2D rendering context with that of webGL2 I am running both versions in to separate canvases, randomly choosing which is ran first, each frame (with added instrumentation):
```js
    drawBalls(structSize, arrayPointer, count) {
        // Call functions in random order
        if (Math.random() < 0.5) {
            var t0 = performance.now();
            drawBalls2d    (structSize, arrayPointer, count);
            var t1 = performance.now();
            drawBallsWebGL2(structSize, arrayPointer, count);
            var t2 = performance.now();

            // For chrome
            console.timeStamp("2d"   , t0, t1, "2d"   , "draw balls");
            console.timeStamp("webgl", t1, t2, "webgl", "draw balls");
            // For firefox
            performance.measure("2d"    , {start: t0, end: t1});
            performance.measure("webgl2", {start: t1, end: t2});
        } else {
            var t0 = performance.now();
            drawBallsWebGL2(structSize, arrayPointer, count);
            var t1 = performance.now();
            drawBalls2d    (structSize, arrayPointer, count);
            var t2 = performance.now();

            // For chrome
            console.timeStamp("webgl", t0, t1, "webgl", "draw balls");
            console.timeStamp("2d"   , t1, t2, "2d"   , "draw balls");
            // For firefox
            performance.measure("webgl2", {start: t0, end: t1});
            performance.measure("2d"    , {start: t1, end: t2});
        }
        ++frameNo;
    }
```
https://github.com/0scarB/balls-collision-demo/blob/1a3ebe13fd2c8617eeac496f481be802977febf2/index.js#L172-L199

I'll prefix the following by saying that I don't expect the Canvas 2D API to run as fast as WebGL, my focus is still on assignment to `.strokeStyle`/`.fillStyle` becoming a bottleneck in situations such as this collision demo.

On both Chrome and Firefox, on my laptop, the Canvas 2D API rendering code is more than 10x slower than the WebGL2 code. In Chrome I measured 68% CPU vs. 1.2% time spent in the respective rendering functions:
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b286eb6b-8d01-4b79-a939-bed336b176a0)
the majority of time is consumed to assigning to `.strokeStyle`:
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7173079d-d0dd-4991-9339-fceae7859d31)
1523ms in total, as compared to the neighboring low-millisecond line times.
In Firefox setting `.strokeStyle` takes up 38% of the CPU time, on average, per frame:
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/76d6d76a-5998-4961-b4d7-8e0b4afe61e9)
whereas the WebGL2 code takes 1.1%:
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1fd43bc1-b8f2-4d79-9e41-398718fbd835)

Seeing as the Canvas 2D API is slower than WebGL in cases such as mine, I think it is worth considering speeding it up where possible. My guess is still that having alternative methods for changing the color such as `setStrokeColorRGB(<number>, <number>, <number>)`, `setFillColorRGB(...)` could be implemented by browsers more efficiently than the current CSS string parsing and validation needed when assigning to `strokeStyle` and `fillStyle`.

MDN mentions the non-normative `setFillColor` https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CanvasRenderingContext2D/fillStyle#webkitblink-specific_note and `setStrokeColor` https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CanvasRenderingContext2D/strokeStyle#webkitblink-specific_note implemented by Blink and Webkit based browsers. It didn't seem to be supported any longer in my version of Chrome so I don't have measurements for comparison, but is possibly a result of browser-implementers coming to similar conclusions in the past, and it might be useful to refer back to surrounding discussions in this context.

domenic's rationale for closing this issue
> I don't think string-parsing is a significant overhead here, given that it's used for many high-performance parts of the web platform.

is likely somewhat premature in this context. The assessment can be reanalysed by comparing the total set of algorithm steps specified to parse and validate assignment values when setting `.strokeStyle` and `.fillStyle`, in the current standard, to shorter algorithms that could be specified for simpler alternatives. For example, here's some vague pseudocode for  `setStrokeColorRGB`:
```
setStrokeColorRGB(r, g, b) {
      // Step 1.: Validate input types
      if (typeof r != typeof g != typeof b != number) throw error; 

       // Step 2.: Truncate values to be in correct range
       r = r & 255;
       g = g & 255;
       b = b & 255;

       // Step 3.: Rendering logic
}
```

PS.: Hacking on Blink and/or Gecko and/or Webkit to implement a prototype of `setStrokeColorRGB` would give better baseline measurements than WebGL2, but is likely significantly more time intensive
